@meta
id: 06-02
title: Zero versus caret
chapter: 06
type: lesson
skills: line-start
par: 18
minutes: 4
vscode: Home once for the text, Home twice for the margin
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
The refund call inside `cancel` is not awaited. If the payment service is
slow, the method returns before the money moves, and the caller is told the
cancellation succeeded. Add the missing keyword so the call is awaited.

The word goes at the start of the code on that line — not at the start of
the line. Those are four spaces apart, and the grader can tell the
difference.

@keys
| `0` | column one, indentation included |
| `^` | the first character on the line that is not a space or tab |
| `I` | insert before the first non-blank — `^` and `i` fused into one key |

@hints
`0` is the wrong tool here: it would put the keyword in the margin, outside
the indentation.
--
`^` stops at the first real character on the line, however deep the
indentation goes.
--
`2j` gets you to the refund call. Then `^`, then `i`, then type the keyword
and a space, then `Esc`.

@solution
    2j ^ i await  <Esc> :wq<Enter>

`I` would have done the same in one key fewer — it is exactly `^` followed
by `i`, which is why chapter 3 could teach it before you had met `^`.

The distinction to keep is this. `0` is a column. `^` is the code. In a file
indented two, four or eight spaces, `^` lands on the same character every
time while `0` lands in the whitespace, and almost everything you want to do
in a source file happens at the code.

`0` still earns its place: column one is where a diff marker, a Markdown
bullet or a git conflict marker lives, and it is the reliable reset when the
line has left you confused. Reach for `^` by default in code and `0` when
you genuinely mean the margin.

@start order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    this.paymentService.refund(id);
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    await this.paymentService.refund(id);
  }
}
